            <div xml:id="d320">

                <pb facs="0388" n="376" sameAs="frus:frus1941v02/pg376"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/12051: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Algiers</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Cole</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Algiers</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-06-13T17:00:00+01:00">June 13, 1941—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 14—8:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>240. For the Under Secretary from Murphy. Your 133, June 10. Weygand returned
                    from Dakar unexpectedly last evening. I saw him <pb facs="0389" n="377"
                        xml:id="pg377"/> shortly after his arrival and conveyed your message. He
                    expressed his gratification.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>General said he had returned to Algiers prior to schedule because he
                        thought it preferable, with the rapidly changing situation, to be in
                        Algiers. He said there was no change in his status.</item>
                    <label>[2.]</label>
                    <item>We talked further about Syria and the General insisted that it is folly
                        for the British to continue in Syria and incite Frenchmen to fight other
                        Frenchmen. He had just concluded a conference with General Odic commanding
                        the air force here. I suspect, but can obtain no confirmation, that the
                        General is faced with a Vichy demand to transfer aviation equipment and
                        personnel to Syria. [Murphy.]</item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cole</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
